Plagiocephaly
Paediatric & Neonatal
Occipital flattening, Ipsilateral frontal bossing, Ear displacement

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-041
Reason for Visit: Caregiver alarm regarding an asymmetric, abnormally shaped head in an infant requiring urgent evaluation to rule out premature cranial suture fusion (craniosynostosis).
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a high level of parental distress after suddenly recognizing significant cranial asymmetry and facial distortion in a 4-month-old infant. Immediate assessment was required to evaluate cranial suture mobility, differentiate positional deformation from true synostosis, and provide immediate reassurance and positioning strategies prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "His head is completely flat on the back right side, and his right ear is pushed forward toward his face."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with distinct, asymmetrical flattening strictly localized to the occipital region of the skull, which the parents state has become more obvious over the past month. The parents report a strong association with prolonged supine infant positioning and exclusive back-sleeping. There is a complete lack of severe underlying neurological deficits, abnormal eye movements, or developmental brain abnormalities. The infant is meeting all appropriate motor and social milestones. Mild ipsilateral neck stiffness was reported by the mother, suggesting concurrent mild congenital muscular torticollis limiting the infant's ability to turn the head to the left while supine.
Functional Status: Active, feeding well, normal developmental trajectory.
Social/Safety Concerns: High caregiver anxiety regarding permanent cosmetic deformity and potential brain compression.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 | HR: 120 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 26 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 6.5 Kg
General: Alert, interactive, symmetrically moving all limbs, afebrile.
Physical Exam: Head inspection from directly above the vertex reveals a distinct parallelogram shape of the skull. There is prominent right occipital flattening accompanied by ipsilateral (right) frontal bossing visibly bulging forward directly above the eye. There is unilateral anterior displacement of the right ear, effectively aligning it closer to the face than the left ear. Direct manual palpation of the cranium reveals normal, completely open, and distinctly mobile lambdoid, coronal, and sagittal sutures. The anterior fontanelle is soft, flat, and open. Assessment of the cervical spine reveals mild tightness of the right sternocleidomastoid muscle. The diagnostic tetrad of occipital flattening, ipsilateral frontal bossing, and ear displacement is confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Infant lying supine in a mechanical rocker with head turned exclusively to the right.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Deformational Plagiocephaly (Positional) - Status: Active/Moderate
Differentials:

Lambdoid Craniosynostosis - Ruled out explicitly by the ipsilateral anterior ear displacement (in synostosis, the ear is pulled posteriorly toward the fused suture) and the palpably open, mobile cranial sutures.

Space-Occupying Intracranial Lesion - Ruled out by the normal anterior fontanelle, absence of focal neurological deficits, and normal developmental milestones.

Microcephaly - Ruled out by a measured fronto-occipital head circumference plotting normally on the 50th percentile for age.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk: Low, Cosmetic risk: Moderate)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The parents' acute distress regarding their infant's rapidly evolving cranial asymmetry necessitated an after-hours assessment to secure an accurate morphological diagnosis. Telephone triage correctly identified the critical need to exclude craniosynostosis, a surgical condition that can restrict brain growth. Objective clinical evaluation explicitly confirmed deformational plagiocephaly driven by positional preference and mild torticollis. The presence of anterior ear displacement on the flattened side definitively ruled out lambdoid synostosis. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in profound, unnecessary parental terror regarding brain damage. Immediate physical evaluation provided absolute diagnostic clarity and safely guided initial conservative mechanical management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the benign, cosmetic nature of the condition and the absence of brain compression. Generated a non-urgent referral to pediatric physiotherapy to address the mild torticollis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To actively encourage the infant to turn their head to the non-preferred side while awake in the cot, caregivers must exclusively place high-value, brightly colored toys and direct all their social interaction (talking/smiling) strictly from the left side. By directly rewarding the physical effort of turning the head left with social and visual stimulation, the infant actively and continuously stretches the tight neck muscle and offloads the flattened occiput.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Pediatric physiotherapy.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to monitor cranial symmetry progress and discuss potential orthotic helmet therapy if no improvement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the infant develops a bulging soft spot on top of the head, begins vomiting forcefully, exhibits abnormal eye movements, or if the skull develops a hard, raised ridge along the suture lines.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Final diagnosis of complex skull shapes may occasionally require formal radiographic confirmation if progress stalls.
Clinical Course: Positional plagiocephaly is purely cosmetic and does not affect brain development. The head shape will slowly round out as the infant begins rolling and sitting independently.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return to the GP if a hard, bony ridge develops along the back or top of the baby's head, or if the baby starts vomiting regularly.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The baby's skull bones are currently soft and moldable, like clay. Because the baby prefers to sleep with their head turned to the right, that side has flattened against the mattress, pushing the ear and forehead forward. It is not fused, and the brain is perfectly safe.
Actionable Steps: Continue "Back to Sleep" to prevent SIDS. Maximize awake "Tummy Time" to take pressure off the back of the head. Alternate the direction the baby lies in the cot so they must look the other way to see the door.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Positional Plagiocephaly (Flat Head Syndrome). Sutures open, brain safe. Start aggressive tummy time. Physio referral sent for tight neck.

SMS EDUCATION

Interact with the baby only from their non-preferred side to encourage head turning. Maximize tummy time. Go to ED for forceful vomiting.
